# Rank 1395 / CVE-2023-52139 (GHSA-7pxq-6xx9-xpgm, CWE-285):
# misskey-dev/misskey issues third-party applications OAuth-style access tokens
# whose granted scopes are a list of permission strings (`read:account`,
# `write:notes`, ...), and each API endpoint declares the one scope it requires in
# its own descriptor as `meta.kind`. `ApiCallService.call` is the single place that
# enforces the pairing, and it enforced it like this
# (packages/backend/src/server/api/ApiCallService.ts:332 at c96bc36f^):
#
#   if (token && ep.meta.kind && !token.permission.some(p => p === ep.meta.kind)) {
#     throw new ApiError({ code: 'PERMISSION_DENIED', ... });
#   }
#
# The endpoint's own requirement declaration is a *conjunct of the guard that
# enforces it*. An endpoint that declares no `kind` therefore skips scope
# enforcement entirely, and the preceding guards in the same method only establish
# that *a user* is authenticated (`requireCredential`) or holds a role
# (`requireModerator` / `requireAdmin`) -- never that the application acting on
# that user's behalf was granted anything. So any third-party app token, whatever
# scopes the user actually approved, could call every privileged endpoint that
# happened to omit `kind`: 70 of the 357 endpoint descriptors at c96bc36f^,
# including `i/delete-account`, `i/update-email`, `i/revoke-token`,
# `i/authorized-apps`, `i/move` and `i/import-following`. The weakness is not a
# missing check. The check is present, correct, and conditioned on the presence of
# the very policy field it consults -- an authorization decision that fails open
# on an unset requirement.
#
# c96bc36f ("Merge pull request from GHSA-7pxq-6xx9-xpgm") closes it by denying
# instead of skipping when the declaration is absent:
#
#   if (token && ((ep.meta.kind && !token.permission.some(p => p === ep.meta.kind))
#     || (!ep.meta.kind && (ep.meta.requireCredential || ep.meta.requireModerator || ep.meta.requireAdmin)))) {
#
# and hardens three other surfaces of the same model: `IEndpointMeta` becomes a
# discriminated union in which `requireCredential: true`, `requireModerator: true`
# and `requireAdmin: true` each *imply* a `kind`, so the omission becomes a type
# error; the admin scopes are split from two coarse values (`read:admin`,
# `write:admin`) into 44 per-area ones; and the streaming endpoint, which had no
# app-scope check at all, gains `if (app !== null && !app.permission.some(p => p
# === 'read:account')) throw new AuthenticationError(...)`.
#
# The pool row is the GHSA merge commit and its metadata reads COMMIT_OK. `git
# show` does print the combined diff here (148 files), so this is not the
# empty-combined-diff symptom; the row is correct, the repository is correct, and
# the vulnerable guard is present verbatim at the parent. No corpus correction.
# The recorded CWE-285 is right for once.
#
# What fires today. The whole tree scans inside the bound -- `timeout 180
# ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB` covers all 1809 JavaScript/TypeScript
# files plus 103 config and 1439 textpattern files -- and reports 613 findings and
# 74 flags on the vulnerable checkout, 613 findings and 78 flags on the patched
# one, both stable over three `--cache off` runs. Nothing on either side addresses
# this weakness. One finding does land inside the vulnerable guard:
# VYQL-CRY-006 reports code.SecretComparisonReview at ApiCallService.ts:333, which
# is `p === ep.meta.kind` read as a secret compared with `===` -- a known
# precision defect, not coverage, and it fires identically after the fix. The four
# extra flags on the patched tree are code.ResourceReview on the socket writes the
# streaming fix adds.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap. The discriminating property of the vulnerable guard is a
# *self-reference*: the expression `ep.meta.kind` appears both as a bare
# truthiness conjunct of the `&&` chain and as the compared-against operand inside
# the negated membership test in the same chain, and nothing anywhere denies when
# it is absent. Nothing in the binding language can express that, because nothing
# in the frontend records it. Four things were measured, each against both
# revisions of the real tree with `-flags only`, emitting
# code.RequestScopePolicyReview (CWE_863, the nearest existing vehicle) from a
# throwaway javascript binding.
#
# 1. The facts available are text, and the two revisions' text differs only in the
#    fix's own field name. Dumping the `analysis.module.context` tokens for a
#    stripped copy of the method on both revisions, the entire difference is four
#    `expr:` tokens. The vulnerable one carries
#    `expr:token&&ep.meta.kind&&!token.permission.some(p=>p===ep.meta.kind)` and
#    `expr:token&&ep.meta.kind`; the patched one carries the same inner
#    `expr:ep.meta.kind&&!token.permission.some(p=>p===ep.meta.kind)` -- verbatim,
#    it is a nested binary_expression and gets its own token -- plus
#    `expr:(...)||(!ep.meta.kind&&(...))`, `expr:!ep.meta.kind&&(...)` and
#    `expr:ep.meta.requireCredential||ep.meta.requireModerator||ep.meta.requireAdmin`.
#    The comparison `p === ep.meta.kind` is a separate `expr:` token with no link
#    to the `.some()` call it is the predicate of, because JavaScript emits no
#    call-argument tokens (rank 1360). So the vulnerable shape's own token is
#    present in the patched file, and any binding keyed on it matches both.
#
# 2. The generic positive half fires *more* after the fix, not less. A
#    function-scoped binding for "a scope-membership test over a token's granted
#    permissions",
#      node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "javascript"
#      and containsAny(node.context.selector, ["token.permission", "token.scopes",
#            "token.scope", "app.permission", "app.scopes",
#            "accessToken.permission", "accessToken.scopes"])
#      and containsAny(node.context.call, ["some", "includes", "indexOf", "has"])
#    reports 3 flags on the vulnerable tree (ApiCallService.ts:223 plus
#    test/e2e/oauth.ts:149 and :186) and 6 on the patched one -- the same three
#    plus StreamingApiServerService.ts:48 and :53 and stream/Connection.ts:244,
#    every one of them a check the *fix* added. Restricting the positive to the
#    fail-open spelling itself,
#      containsAny(node.context.expr, ["&&!token.permission.some(",
#        "&&!token.scopes.includes(", "&&!token.scope.includes(",
#        "&&!app.permission.some("])
#    gives 1 flag on the vulnerable tree and 3 on the patched one: the same
#    ApiCallService.ts:223 on both, identical, plus the two the streaming fix
#    added. The presence of a scope-membership test is evidence of enforcement;
#    the direction of that signal is inverted from what a finding needs, which is
#    the same failure rank 1311 measured when a patched revision scored higher.
#    Two of the three vulnerable-side hits are e2e test fixtures, and per rank
#    1381 `inTestOrSeedFile` is inert, so they cannot be excluded by file path
#    either.
#
# 3. Adding the fail-closed negation does discriminate, and only by naming one
#    application's field. Extending (2) with
#      not containsAny(node.context.expr, ["!ep.meta.kind&&", "!app.permission.some"])
#    reports 1 flag on the vulnerable tree (ApiCallService.ts:223) and 0 on the
#    patched one. That is the measurement, and it is also the reason this is not
#    shippable: `ep.meta.kind` is misskey's own endpoint-descriptor field, not an
#    API anybody could write this binding from documentation for, and a fix
#    spelling the same denial `ep.meta.kind == null` or renaming the field scores
#    as unpatched. AGENTS.md's genericity test refuses it, and section 5 of the
#    accumulated context records four rejections of exactly this move.
#
# 4. The descriptor side cannot carry it either, and costs 70 flags to prove it.
#    The endpoint descriptors are where the omission physically is, so
#      node.scope == "module" and node.context.language == "javascript"
#      and containsAny(node.context.prop, ["requireCredential=true",
#            "requireAdmin=true", "requireModerator=true"])
#      and not containsAny(node.context.prop, ["kind="])
#    is the obvious shape. It reports 70 flags on the vulnerable tree and 39 on
#    the patched one -- the scanner's own count agreeing exactly with a text
#    enumeration of the 357 descriptors. It does not discriminate because the fix
#    did not add `kind` to those endpoints: it made the dispatcher refuse app
#    tokens on them. A descriptor with no scope declaration is not a defect, and
#    39 of them ship in the fixed release. This is the shape section 9 already
#    records as unshippable at ranks 1311 and 1331, re-measured on a fifth
#    codebase.
#
# Two known JavaScript/TypeScript gaps were checked and are *not* what blocks
# this. Rank 1381's `module.exports = class X` gap does not apply: misskey writes
# `export class ApiCallService implements OnApplicationShutdown`, and `vyql query
# -type Call` on the repro reports `analysis.function.context` for the method at
# its own line, so function-scoped facts exist. Rank 1377's validator-returns-only-
# an-error gap does not apply either: this guard throws.
#
# Behaviour a shippable fact needs. In
# internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go, the `binary_expression` case
# of `jsStructuredContextTokens` emits only `expr:<compacted text>`. What is
# missing is a structural fact over one `&&` chain: when a conjunct is a bare
# member-access read `X` (no comparison, no negation) and another conjunct of the
# same chain is a negated membership call whose compared operand is textually `X`,
# and no branch of the enclosing condition tests `!X` or `X == null`, emit one
# boolean -- `fail_open_policy_declaration_guard=true`, in the register the file
# already uses for `prototype_key_guard=true`, `own_property_key_guard=true` and
# `path_segment_type_guard=true`. Keying the fact on the relation rather than on
# `X`'s spelling is the whole point: a binding can then require the fail-open
# form and pair it with a documented permission-collection API without knowing
# what any application calls its policy field. This is not mechanical -- it is a
# new classification and a new relation between sibling conjuncts, not a missing
# switch case -- so nothing is fixed here. It shares its root with rank 1360 (no
# call-argument tokens, which is why the `.some()` predicate's comparison is an
# orphan token) and rank 1373 (`expr:` is in flagASTRoutingValuePrefixes, so it
# routes AST-wide with a case-sensitive substring fallback rather than staying on
# the node).
#
# Vocabulary was checked for reuse before anything was written. No concept and no
# rule ID is minted, because nothing discriminating can be built on top of one.
# code.OptionalTokenClaimValidation (VYQL-AUTH-118, rank 1373) is the closest
# relative and is the mirror image of this: there the *token* supplies a claim the
# validator treats as optional, and its Go binding names the issuer claim on both
# sides of the comparison; here the *server* supplies the requirement and the
# token side is fully present. code.RequestScopePolicyReview is the review vehicle
# the measurements above borrow, not a fit -- it is about request and resource
# metadata used without a scope comparison, and a comparison is exactly what this
# code does. code.MemberStoreMissingCreateAuthorization,
# code.FlaskEntityDetailMissingAuthorization and
# code.WordPressMediaMutationMissingObjectPermission are all "handler with no
# authorization signal", which section 9 records as measured unshippable.
#
# The three cases below are the minimal pair plus the inverted-signal case, all
# three rejected today. The first two differ only in whether the guard denies when
# the endpoint declares no required scope. The third is the streaming handshake
# check the fix *added*, which every candidate binding in (2) flags -- a check
# introduced as remediation, scored as the defect.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, all three forms rejected.

test "rank1395: an API dispatcher enforces an app token's scope only when the endpoint declares one, so a privileged endpoint that declares none skips the check"
  lang javascript
  file packages/backend/src/server/api/ApiCallService.ts
  reject_review code.RequestScopePolicyReview
  code
  ```
  import type { IEndpoint } from './endpoints.js';

  export class ApiCallService implements OnApplicationShutdown {
    private async call(
      ep: IEndpoint,
      token: MiAccessToken | null,
      user: MiLocalUser | null | undefined,
      data: any,
      file?: { name: string; path: string },
    ) {
      if (ep.meta.requireCredential && user == null) {
        throw new ApiError({
          message: 'Credential required.',
          code: 'CREDENTIAL_REQUIRED',
          kind: 'permission',
          id: '1384574d-a912-4b81-8601-c7b1c4085df1',
        });
      }

      if (token && ep.meta.secure) {
        throw new ApiError({
          message: 'The request requires an accessible credential.',
          code: 'ACCESS_DENIED',
          kind: 'permission',
          id: '56f35758-7dd5-468b-8439-5d6fb8ec9b8e',
        });
      }

      if ((ep.meta.requireModerator || ep.meta.requireAdmin) && !user!.isRoot) {
        const myRoles = await this.roleService.getUserRoles(user!.id);
        if (ep.meta.requireModerator && !myRoles.some(r => r.isModerator || r.isAdministrator)) {
          throw new ApiError({
            message: 'You are not assigned to a moderator role.',
            code: 'ROLE_PERMISSION_DENIED',
            kind: 'permission',
            id: 'd33d5333-db36-423d-a8f9-1a2b9549da41',
          });
        }
        if (ep.meta.requireAdmin && !myRoles.some(r => r.isAdministrator)) {
          throw new ApiError({
            message: 'You are not assigned to an administrator role.',
            code: 'ROLE_PERMISSION_DENIED',
            kind: 'permission',
            id: 'c3d38592-54c0-429d-be96-5636b0431a61',
          });
        }
      }

      if (token && ep.meta.kind && !token.permission.some(p => p === ep.meta.kind)) {
        throw new ApiError({
          message: 'Your app does not have the necessary permissions to use this endpoint.',
          code: 'PERMISSION_DENIED',
          kind: 'permission',
          id: '1370e5b7-d4eb-4566-bb1d-7748ee6a1838',
        });
      }

      return await ep.exec(data, user, token, file, request.ip, request.headers);
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1395: the same dispatcher denying an app token outright when a privileged endpoint declares no required scope"
  lang javascript
  file packages/backend/src/server/api/ApiCallService.ts
  reject_review code.RequestScopePolicyReview
  code
  ```
  import type { IEndpoint } from './endpoints.js';

  export class ApiCallService implements OnApplicationShutdown {
    private async call(
      ep: IEndpoint,
      token: MiAccessToken | null,
      user: MiLocalUser | null | undefined,
      data: any,
      file?: { name: string; path: string },
    ) {
      if (ep.meta.requireCredential && user == null) {
        throw new ApiError({
          message: 'Credential required.',
          code: 'CREDENTIAL_REQUIRED',
          kind: 'permission',
          id: '1384574d-a912-4b81-8601-c7b1c4085df1',
        });
      }

      if (token && ep.meta.secure) {
        throw new ApiError({
          message: 'The request requires an accessible credential.',
          code: 'ACCESS_DENIED',
          kind: 'permission',
          id: '56f35758-7dd5-468b-8439-5d6fb8ec9b8e',
        });
      }

      if ((ep.meta.requireModerator || ep.meta.requireAdmin) && !user!.isRoot) {
        const myRoles = await this.roleService.getUserRoles(user!.id);
        if (ep.meta.requireModerator && !myRoles.some(r => r.isModerator || r.isAdministrator)) {
          throw new ApiError({
            message: 'You are not assigned to a moderator role.',
            code: 'ROLE_PERMISSION_DENIED',
            kind: 'permission',
            id: 'd33d5333-db36-423d-a8f9-1a2b9549da41',
          });
        }
        if (ep.meta.requireAdmin && !myRoles.some(r => r.isAdministrator)) {
          throw new ApiError({
            message: 'You are not assigned to an administrator role.',
            code: 'ROLE_PERMISSION_DENIED',
            kind: 'permission',
            id: 'c3d38592-54c0-429d-be96-5636b0431a61',
          });
        }
      }

      if (token && ((ep.meta.kind && !token.permission.some(p => p === ep.meta.kind))
        || (!ep.meta.kind && (ep.meta.requireCredential || ep.meta.requireModerator || ep.meta.requireAdmin)))) {
        throw new ApiError({
          message: 'Your app does not have the necessary permissions to use this endpoint.',
          code: 'PERMISSION_DENIED',
          kind: 'permission',
          id: '1370e5b7-d4eb-4566-bb1d-7748ee6a1838',
        });
      }

      return await ep.exec(data, user, token, file, request.ip, request.headers);
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1395: a streaming handshake that rejects an application token lacking the minimum scope"
  lang javascript
  file packages/backend/src/server/api/StreamingApiServerService.ts
  reject_review code.RequestScopePolicyReview
  code
  ```
  export class StreamingApiServerService {
    private attach(server: http.Server): void {
      this.#wss = new WebSocket.Server({ noServer: true });

      server.on('upgrade', async (request, socket, head) => {
        const q = new URL(request.url!, `http://${request.headers.host}`).searchParams;
        const token = q.get('i');

        let user: MiLocalUser | null = null;
        let app: MiAccessToken | null = null;

        try {
          [user, app] = await this.authenticateService.authenticate(token);

          if (app !== null && !app.permission.some(p => p === 'read:account')) {
            throw new AuthenticationError('Your app does not have necessary permissions to use websocket API.');
          }
        } catch (e) {
          if (e instanceof AuthenticationError) {
            socket.write([
              'HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized',
              'WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm="Misskey", error="invalid_token"',
            ].join('\r\n') + '\r\n\r\n');
          } else {
            socket.write('HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error\r\n\r\n');
          }
          socket.destroy();
          return;
        }

        this.#wss.handleUpgrade(request, socket, head, (ws) => {
          this.#wss.emit('connection', ws, request, { user, app });
        });
      });
    }
  }
  ```
